# Who the governance gate actually asks.
#
# The release workflows talk about approval by governance, and until this file
# existed that was a description of an intention rather than a rule anything
# enforced. A required review from a code owner is what turns it into one.
#
# Two things still have to be set in the GitHub UI, because a file in the repo
# cannot grant itself authority:
#
#   Settings > Branches > main: require a pull request, require the `verify`
#   check, and refuse force pushes and deletion. Nothing in CI pushes to `main`,
#   by design, so there is no bot that needs an exemption.
#
#   Settings > Environments > experimental: required reviewers. That is the
#   gate on publishing from a pull request, and it is the one that works for
#   forks, where a workflow gets no secrets and a label proves nothing.
#
# **Two of those have moved since this was written, and both are worth knowing
# before anyone sets them.**
#
# This file used to name `verify` and `browser-specs` as the checks to require.
# A branch rule matches a *job* name and not a workflow name, and 274 sharded
# `verify.yml` into `plan`, `checks (0)` through `checks (5)`, `lint`, `types`,
# `tests` and four `browser` entries, so neither name existed any more and
# requiring either would have blocked every pull request on a check that never
# reports. `verify.yml` now ends in an aggregation job called `verify` that
# needs all of them: one context to require, immune to a shard count or a
# renamed script, and it is why the line above is true again.
#
# Required reviewers on an environment returned HTTP 422 on 26 Aug 2026,
# *"ensure the billing plan supports the required reviewers protection rule"*,
# against an organization on the Team plan. The setting is available once the
# repository is public, which 272 is about, so this closes with that flip rather
# than before it. Until then `release-experimental.yml` describes an approval
# that nothing enforces, and that is the state, not an oversight.
#
# Today this is one person, and naming that plainly is better than a team that
# does not exist yet. **Required review is therefore off rather than on**:
# GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull request, so requiring one
# with a single code owner makes `main` unmergeable except by admin bypass,
# which is a gate that exists only by being ignored. Required approvals go to 1
# and `require_code_owner_reviews` goes on the day there is a second person.
# Replace the owner with @project-fusion/governance then too.

